<!--
  Litestone page § Tenants and secrets. Facts: packages/litestone/docs/
  multi-tenancy.md (both strategies, the row desugar into @@deny, per-tenant
  keys), docs/encryption.md (@encrypted, @hashed, @guarded, @secret,
  $rotateKey and its orphan refusal), docs/trail.md § Protected fields are
  redacted and § Personal data, and the data-hazards entry on FJS-716 (a
  failed decrypt is raised, naming the key wanted and the key held).
  The proof is a real run: the schema and script sit in the session scratch
  directory, and every output line is verbatim except the error, cut at its
  first sentence. A second run proved strategy database with a key per
  tenant: one tenant's file opened with another tenant's key raised
  DecryptionFailedError.
  Not claimed: a cross-tenant update or delete is not refused by name, it
  answers null and changes nothing, so the point says "changes nothing".
  Nothing here says the trail never holds a secret in general: redaction
  covers the declared attributes, and an undeclared column logs in full.
-->
<script>
  const PROOF = `// schema.lite
tenancy { strategy row  column workspaceId  claim workspaceId }
database audit { path "./audit/"  driver trail }

model Customer {
  id          Int     @id
  workspaceId Int
  name        String
  ssn         String? @encrypted
  apiKey      String? @secret
  loginToken  String? @hashed
  @@trail(audit)
}

$ bun proof.mjs
workspace 10 reads [{"id":1,"workspaceId":10,"name":"Ada"}]
workspace 20 reads []
workspace 20 writes into 10: AccessDeniedError Outside your workspaceId
trail {"operation":"create","records":[1],"actorId":"u1","after":{"id":1,"workspaceId":10,"name":"Ada","ssn":"[redacted]","loginToken":"[redacted]"}}
on disk {"ssn":"v2.59514c9d.4SmPTqFvY5NaOZVlILshUxMA2awKA-AWf_c9VkCix0Qf-2KCb6dP","loginToken":"v2h.59514c9d.4BaG8y-pPHcKZ-hIl0bcIi1Z5BINl2K-4YvL780H43k"}
another key: DecryptionFailedError Customer.ssn: Cannot decrypt: the value names key '59514c9d' and this client holds 'bdd16940'`
</script>

<section id="tenants" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Tenants and secrets</p>
    <h2>Each workspace sees only its own rows. The audit trail keeps no protected value.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      One block in the schema says what a tenant is, and one attribute on a field says what a
      stolen file or a log line may give away. It all rests on a key you keep yourself.
    </p>

    <div class="bc-split">
      <ul class="bc-points">
        <li>
          <strong>A workspace is a wall.</strong>
          With <code>strategy row</code>, another workspace's rows read as empty, an update across
          the line changes nothing, and a create into it is refused by name.
          <code>strategy database</code> gives each tenant its own SQLite file instead, and can
          give each its own key.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Three attributes, three promises.</strong>
          <code>@encrypted</code> stores AES-256-GCM ciphertext that only the system reads back,
          and <code>@hashed</code> stores a one-way HMAC-SHA256 you can match in a
          <code>where</code> but nobody can read. <code>@guarded</code> encrypts nothing: it makes
          a column system-only for reads and writes, and <code>@secret</code> is
          <code>@encrypted</code> plus <code>@guarded</code>, its writes logged wherever the schema
          declares a trail.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The trail records that, not what.</strong>
          A protected field's entry says who wrote which rows and when, with the value as
          <code>[redacted]</code>, and a <code>@personal</code> column logs as
          <code>[personal]</code>. The other fields on the row are kept in full.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lose the key and the data is gone.</strong>
          An encrypted value reads back only under the key that wrote it, and nothing in
          Litestone can recover it without that key. <code>$rotateKey</code> moves every
          encrypted column to a new key, and refuses while a <code>@hashed</code> column would be
          stranded under the old one.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="bc-proof">
        <pre class="code">{PROOF}</pre>
        <figcaption>
          A run of Litestone's own client against this schema, under two principals in two
          workspaces. The output is verbatim, the last line cut at its first sentence:
          <code>on disk</code> is the column read straight from SQLite, and
          <code>another key</code> is the same file opened with a different key.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="bc-more">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/multi-tenancy.md">the two tenancy strategies</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/encryption.md">encrypted, hashed, guarded and secret</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/encryption.md#key-rotation">key rotation</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/trail.md#protected-fields-are-redacted">what the trail redacts</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .bc-proof pre.code { overflow-x: auto; font-size: .82rem; }
</style>
